{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES REQUESTED at exact pushed head 4b93b6d82a47c8adb8717abe14fd3084f9fcdc2f. Independent verification on a clean detached checkout; gates re-run there: gofmt empty, go build, go vet, git diff --check, go test ./spike/... -count=1 -v all pass, full go test ./... -count=1 (35 packages ok). Diff scope vs merge-base 3371b6c5c is exactly the ten cited spike paths; nothing outside spike/. ARCHITECTURE: spike tree only; no kernel, host-log, Workroom or application-format contract changes; profile.go was untouched on main between the branch base and the merge-in, so the merge commit resolved nothing and spikes one, two and three pass together at fold version @1. SECURITY: loopback binding enforced (main.go:66-79, rejects 0.0.0.0), credential of at least 16 bytes compared in constant time and never rendered into the page, ephemeral per-process signer used only under credential on /api/events, 32 KiB body cap with unknown-field refusal, 250 ms query and bounded wait timeouts, server read/write/idle timeouts, and /api/query routed through the pre-existing read-only default-deny authorizer with 16 KiB/256-row/256 KiB bounds; the wait compares and captures its channel under one mutex so no wakeup is missed, and append-then-rebuild failure yields 422 with idempotent retry. Two notes, not blockers: the credential is printed to the server log at startup as its delivery channel (a secret in logs, acceptable on loopback but say so), and /api/query is unauthenticated by design — state that posture in README. Live-reference execution is real: compatibility_test.go:124-142 runs node against the JS source shipped in the pinned Go module and node absence is a hard failure, not a skip. Mutation evidence: changing a fixture reference value fails compatibility_test.go:59; inverting the frontier comparison at inventory_ui.go:285 fails inventory_ui_test.go:47 and :116; removing the change notification at :254 fails inventory_ui_test.go:74; dropping each/keys/spread from the profile guard fails the three order-dependent corpus cases at compatibility_test.go:92. BLOCKER ONE (condition: the four UI steps): the shipped browser flow fails on its own default input. The page textarea default is {\"id\":\"stock-1\",\"sku\":\"ink\",\"qty\":5} (inventory_ui.go:344); decodeEvent (projection.go:602-605) requires the payload to byte-equal json.Marshal of the decoded map, which sorts keys, and the browser JSON.stringify preserves insertion order, so the server answers event payload is not canonically encoded. Verified by running the binary on 127.0.0.1 and driving all four steps with curl: sorted keys succeed, the default order is refused. The Go test passes only because postJSON marshals a map and so sorts keys — it never exercises browser key order. Fix by canonicalising in the page script or server-side before append, and add a test that posts a raw unsorted body. BLOCKER TWO (condition: name every corpus divergence; profile truthfulness): CORPUS.md:29-30 says profile loading rejects object wildcard or descendant traversal, but the guard at profile.go:58 is \\.\\s*\\*{1,2} and matches a wildcard only after a dot; a bare * fold program and a leading **.id both load (probed with a temporary test, deleted), and * over the {meta,event,rows} binding enumerates values in Go map order — exactly the order-dependent class the profile claims to exclude. Either widen the guard to bare and leading wildcards with a corpus case that fails on it, or narrow the CORPUS.md claim to what the guard does. Also the reference-divergence class (compatibility_test.go:94-101, field Go) has zero cases and no CORPUS.md mention. Corpus cross-check otherwise clean: the four order-dependent and three environment-dependent cases the tests tolerate are exactly those CORPUS.md names, and the evaluator limits match the profile. SIMPLIFICATION: remove or populate the empty reference-divergence class; the order-dependent subtests could stop once both map orders are observed instead of always running 64 evaluations. An availability note for the spike record: serveEvent holds the UI mutex across append plus full rebuild, so every event blocks all reads for a replay. The what-the-wait-observes and no-advance results are correct and pinned (200 with the new Gitseq-Frontier on advance; 204 with the unchanged frontier on timeout). Do not merge under this review.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:3fee8c7ac666f9ce5a10c819344b9aa8d5c620a1","head":"4b93b6d82a47c8adb8717abe14fd3084f9fcdc2f","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:78c8ae9dfef2b4d7bfd34d71663de0adea2c10c3\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:00d0f2ac73ee1e7b67abdc5f72e2529e8007cee9","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}